Product Search API — 

What It Is
A single REST endpoint that sits between your frontend and Google Vertex AI Retail Search — one of Google's most advanced product discovery engines used by large-scale e-commerce platforms globally.

POST /api/v1/search
Content-Type: application/json
Your frontend sends a simple JSON body. The API handles all the complexity of authenticating, translating, and calling Google Cloud — and returns a clean, structured response.

Feature 1 — Keyword Search with AI Ranking
The core capability. You send a search term and get back products ranked by Google's AI relevance model.

{
  "query": "red running shoes",
  "visitorId": "user-session-abc123"
}
* query — what the user typed
* visitorId — a session or cookie ID; Google uses this to personalise results over time. The more searches a visitor makes, the better their results get.

Feature 2 — Sorting
Control how results are ordered. The API accepts human-friendly aliases — no need to know Vertex AI's internal expression syntax.

{ "orderBy": "price_low_to_high" }
Alias you send	What it does
relevance	Google AI default — best match first
price_low_to_high	Cheapest first
price_high_to_low	Most expensive first
newest	Recently added first
Any unknown alias passes straight through as a raw Vertex AI orderBy expression, so you're not locked to these four.

Feature 3 — Faceted Navigation (Filters for the Filter Panel)
Ask the API to return counts per attribute alongside results — this is what powers your filter sidebar.

{ "facetKeys": ["color", "brand", "size", "category", "price"] }
Response:

"facets": [
  { "key": "colorFamilies", "values": [{"value":"Red","count":42}, {"value":"Blue","count":35}] },
  { "key": "brands",        "values": [{"value":"Nike","count":58}, {"value":"Adidas","count":47}] }
]
Again, client-friendly aliases — color → colorFamilies, brand → brands etc. All mappings are configurable in appsettings.json without a code change.

Feature 4 — Filtering
Narrow results using Vertex AI's filter expression language. This is what gets applied when a user clicks a facet value.

{ "filter": "availability: IN_STOCK" }
More complex filters are fully supported:

{ "filter": "colorFamilies: ANY(\"Red\", \"Blue\") AND price: IN(50, 150)" }

Feature 5 — Pagination
Two modes — use whichever fits your frontend:
Offset-based (classic page numbers):

{ "pageSize": 20, "offset": 40 }
→ Page 3 of 20 results per page.
Cursor-based (infinite scroll / "load more"):

{ "pageToken": "eyJwYWdlIjogMn0..." }
→ Take the nextPageToken from the previous response and pass it back. More reliable for deep pagination — the cursor encodes exact position and experiment context.
Default page size is 20, maximum is 100 — both configurable.

Feature 6 — Store-Scoped Search
A key feature for Woolworths specifically. Add a storeId and results are scoped to what's available at that physical store, with local pricing applied.

{ "query": "milk", "visitorId": "u1", "storeId": "store-042" }
Without storeId → results from the entire national catalog.
With storeId → only products with local inventory at that store, with that store's price.
Under the hood this maps to Vertex AI's PlaceId field, which uses LocalInventory.place_id for matching.

Feature 7 — Query Expansion
Controls what happens when a search returns very few results.

{ "queryExpansionCondition": "AUTO" }
Value	Behaviour
AUTO (default)	Google AI automatically widens the query when results are sparse
DISABLED	Strict matching only — returns fewer but exact results
Feature 8 — Spell Correction
Fully automatic — no configuration required. If a user types runnnig shoez, Vertex AI corrects it and the response tells you what was used:

{
  "correctedQuery": "running shoes",
  "products": [ ... ]
}
Your frontend can display "Showing results for: running shoes" using this field.

Feature 9 — Full Pricing Information
Every product in the response carries a complete price object:

"price": {
  "currencyCode": "AUD",
  "price": 149.99,
  "originalPrice": 199.99,
  "cost": 89.50,
  "priceEffectiveTime": "2025-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "priceExpireTime":    "2025-12-31T23:59:59Z",
  "priceRange": {
    "priceMin": 139.99,      "priceMax": 169.99,
    "originalPriceMin": 179.99, "originalPriceMax": 219.99
  }
}
* originalPrice — only present when there's a genuine discount (differs from price)
* priceRange — min/max spread across all variants of that product (sizes, colours)
* priceEffectiveTime / priceExpireTime — lets the frontend drive time-limited promotions without any extra logic
* cost — available for margin calculations on internal dashboards
Fields are omitted entirely when not set — no nulls cluttering the response.

Feature 10 — Search Controls & Redirects
When Vertex AI serving controls are active (boost rules, bury rules, redirect rules configured in the GCP console), the response tells you exactly what was applied:

"appliedControls": ["boost-new-arrivals", "pin-sale-items"],
"redirectUri": "https://example.com/sale"
* appliedControls — useful for debugging why a product ranked higher than expected
* redirectUri — if a search rule triggers a full redirect (e.g. "woolworths" → homepage), your frontend reads this and navigates

Feature 11 — Attribution Token
Every response carries an attributionToken:

"attributionToken": "AHRlcnJpZmljLXRva2Vu..."
Pass this back to Google with any click, add-to-cart, or purchase event. It closes the feedback loop — Google learns which results led to conversions and improves future rankings automatically.

Error Handling — Always Consistent
Every error, regardless of source, returns the same shape:

{ "error": "query is required" }
HTTP Status	Cause
400	Missing query or visitorId, invalid pageSize/offset, bad JSON
401	GCP credentials missing or expired
403	Service account missing IAM role
404	Catalog or branch not configured correctly in GCP
429	Rate limit hit (60 requests/60s per IP) or GCP quota exceeded
503	Vertex AI temporarily unavailable
Rate Limiting
60 requests per 60 seconds per client IP, enforced at the API layer before any GCP call is made. Exceeding it returns:

HTTP 429
Retry-After: 45
{ "error": "Too many requests. Please slow down." }

What Goes In / What Comes Out — Full Picture
Request (all fields):

{
  "query":                  "red running shoes",   ← required
  "visitorId":              "session-abc123",       ← required
  "pageSize":               20,
  "offset":                 0,
  "pageToken":              "eyJ...",              ← alternative to offset
  "orderBy":                "price_low_to_high",
  "filter":                 "availability: IN_STOCK",
  "facetKeys":              ["color", "brand", "size"],
  "queryExpansionCondition":"AUTO",
  "storeId":                "store-042"
}
Response:

{
  "products":        [ { "id", "title", "categories", "uri", "attributes", "variants", "price" } ],
  "facets":          [ { "key", "values": [{ "value", "count" }] } ],
  "stats":           { "returned": 20, "totalResults": 157, "offset": 0 },
  "nextPageToken":   "eyJ...",
  "correctedQuery":  "running shoes",
  "attributionToken":"AHRl...",
  "redirectUri":     null,
  "appliedControls": ["boost-new-arrivals"]
}




Search API — Data Flow Across Files

HTTP POST /api/v1/search
         │
         ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│  SearchController   │  Creates SearchContext, fires StepService
└────────┬────────────┘
         │  StepService.ExecuteAsync(context)
         ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│    StepService      │  Calls firstStep.HandleAsync(context)
└────────┬────────────┘
         │  Step 1
         ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│ ValidateRequestStep │  Reads context.ApiRequest, throws on bad input
└────────┬────────────┘
         │  Step 2
         ▼
┌──────────────────────┐
│  ConvertRequestStep  │  Reads ApiRequest → writes RetailSearchRequest
└────────┬─────────────┘
         │  Step 3
         ▼
┌─────────────────────┐
│     SearchStep      │  Reads RetailSearchRequest → calls GCP → writes RetailSearchResponse
└────────┬────────────┘
         │  Step 4
         ▼
┌──────────────────────┐
│ ConvertResponseStep  │  Reads RetailSearchResponse → writes ApiResponse
└────────┬─────────────┘
         │
         ▼
  Returns context.ApiResponse  →  HTTP 200 JSON



  Step 0 — Entry Point
File: Controllers/SearchController.cs


HTTP JSON body  →  KeywordSearchRequest (deserialized by ASP.NET Core)

var context = new SearchContext { ApiRequest = request };
return (await _stepService.ExecuteAsync(context)).ApiResponse!;
Creates a fresh SearchContext with only ApiRequest populated. Hands it to StepService. The SearchContext is the single shared object that all four steps read from and write to.

Context state after this step:


ApiRequest          ✅  KeywordSearchRequest { query, visitorId, pageSize, ... }
RetailSearchRequest ❌  null
RetailSearchResponse❌  null
ApiResponse         ❌  null
Step 0.5 — Pipeline Executor
File: Services/Pipeline/StepService.cs


await _firstStep.HandleAsync(context);
return context;
Knows only the first step of the chain. Calls HandleAsync on it — the chain runs itself after that. Returns the mutated context when all steps are done.

Step 0.6 — Base Step Mechanics
File: Services/Pipeline/Step.cs

Every step inherits from Step<T>. On each call to HandleAsync:

Starts a Stopwatch
Calls InnerHandleAsync(context) — the subclass logic
Logs elapsed time; logs WARNING if > 500ms
Calls _nextStep.HandleAsync(context) — hands off to the next step
This is what makes the chain self-executing — no step knows about the one after, they just call _nextStep.

Step 1 — Validation
File: Services/Pipeline/Search/ValidateRequestStep.cs

Reads: context.ApiRequest

Writes: nothing

Throws: InvalidSearchRequestException → HTTP 400


Checks performed:
  query       → non-blank, required
  visitorId   → non-blank, required
  pageSize    → 1 to MaxPageSize (100) if provided
  offset      → >= 0 if provided
If any check fails the exception bubbles up through GlobalExceptionHandler and the pipeline stops. Nothing reaches GCP.

Context state after this step: unchanged — still only ApiRequest.

Step 2 — Request Translation
Files:

Services/Pipeline/Search/ConvertRequestStep.cs — orchestrates
Services/Mappers/SearchRequestMapper.cs — does the work
Reads: context.ApiRequest

Writes: context.RetailSearchRequest

ConvertRequestStep calls:


context.RetailSearchRequest = _mapper.ToRetailSearchRequest(context.ApiRequest!);
SearchRequestMapper translates field by field:


KeywordSearchRequest          →    Google SearchRequest (protobuf)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
query                         →    Query
visitorId                     →    VisitorId
pageSize ?? 20                →    PageSize
pageToken (if set)            →    PageToken   (offset ignored)
offset    (if no token)       →    Offset
filter                        →    Filter
storeId                       →    PlaceId
orderBy   "price_low_to_high" →    OrderBy "price"        (SortMap alias)
facetKeys ["color","brand"]   →    FacetSpecs [colorFamilies, brands]  (FacetKeys alias)
queryExpansionCondition       →    QueryExpansionSpec.Condition (enum parse, fallback AUTO)
(from config)                 →    Branch    "projects/.../branches/default_branch"
(from config)                 →    Placement "projects/.../servingConfigs/default_search"
Context state after this step:


ApiRequest           ✅  KeywordSearchRequest
RetailSearchRequest  ✅  Google.Cloud.Retail.V2.SearchRequest (protobuf)
RetailSearchResponse ❌  null
ApiResponse          ❌  null
Step 3 — GCP Call
File: Services/Pipeline/Search/SearchStep.cs

Reads: context.RetailSearchRequest

Writes: context.RetailSearchResponse


context.RetailSearchResponse = await _client
    .SearchAsync(context.RetailSearchRequest!)
    .AsRawResponses()
    .FirstAsync();
SearchServiceClient is a singleton — gRPC connection was warm-started at application startup
.AsRawResponses().FirstAsync() — gets the first page of the paginated gRPC stream as a single SearchResponse
Any exception is caught, logged, and re-thrown as SearchServiceException → HTTP 503
Context state after this step:


ApiRequest           ✅  KeywordSearchRequest
RetailSearchRequest  ✅  Google SearchRequest (protobuf)
RetailSearchResponse ✅  Google SearchResponse (protobuf) — raw from GCP
ApiResponse          ❌  null
Step 4 — Response Translation
Files:

Services/Pipeline/Search/ConvertResponseStep.cs — orchestrates
Services/Mappers/SearchResponseMapper.cs — does the work
Reads: context.RetailSearchResponse + context.RetailSearchRequest (for resolved offset)

Writes: context.ApiResponse

SearchResponseMapper.ToApiResponse maps:


Google SearchResponse (protobuf)       →    KeywordSearchResponse
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
response.Results[]                     →    Products[]
  result.Product.PrimaryProductId      →      id  (fallback: Product.Id → result.Id)
  product.Title                        →      title
  product.Categories                   →      categories[]
  product.Uri                          →      uri
  product.Attributes (CustomAttribute) →      attributes { key: "text1, text2" }
  product.Variants[].Id                →      variants[{ id }]
  product.PriceInfo                    →      price { currencyCode, price, originalPrice,
                                                       cost, priceEffectiveTime,
                                                       priceExpireTime, priceRange }
response.Facets[]                      →    Facets[]
  facet.Key                            →      key
  facet.Values[].Value + .Count        →      values[{ value, count }]
response.TotalSize + products.Count    →    Stats { returned, totalResults, offset }
response.NextPageToken                 →    NextPageToken
response.CorrectedQuery                →    CorrectedQuery
response.AttributionToken              →    AttributionToken
response.RedirectUri                   →    RedirectUri
response.AppliedControls[]             →    AppliedControls[]
Context state after this step (final):


ApiRequest           ✅  KeywordSearchRequest
RetailSearchRequest  ✅  Google SearchRequest (protobuf)
RetailSearchResponse ✅  Google SearchResponse (protobuf)
ApiResponse          ✅  KeywordSearchResponse  ← this is returned to the caller
Return Path
Back in SearchController:


return (await _stepService.ExecuteAsync(context)).ApiResponse!;